= #text("A Defence of Nonsense / 为胡说八道辩护")

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("There are two equal and eternal ways of looking at this twilight world of ours: we may see it as the twilight of evening or the twilight of morning; we may think of anything, down to a fallen acorn, as a descendant or as an ancestor. There are times when we are almost crushed, not so much with the load of the evil as with the load of the goodness of humanity, when we feel that we are nothing but the inheritors of a humiliating splendour. But there are other times when everything seems primitive, when the ancient stars are only sparks blown from a boy's bonfire, when the whole earth seems so young and experimental that even the white hair of the aged, in the fine biblical phrase, is like almond-trees that blossom, like the white hawthorn grown in May. That it is good for a man to realize that he is 'the heir of all the ages' is pretty commonly admitted; it is a less popular but equally important point that it is good for him sometimes to realize that he is not only an ancestor, but an ancestor of primal antiquity; it is good for him to wonder whether he is not a hero, and to experience ennobling doubts as to whether he is not a solar myth.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("有两种平等且永恒的方式来看待我们这个黄昏的世界：我们可以将其视为傍晚的黄昏或早晨的黄昏；我们可以将任何事物视为后代或祖先，小至掉落的橡子。有时，我们几乎被压垮，与其说是被邪恶的重担压垮，不如说是被人性善良的重压压垮了，此时我们感到自己只不过是屈辱辉煌的继承者。但也有一些时候，一切都显得原始，古老的星星只是男孩篝火中喷出的火花，整个地球显得如此年轻和实验，甚至连老年人的白发，用圣经里优美的短语来说，都像开花的杏树，像五月长出的白色山楂。人们普遍承认，一个人认识到自己是“万世的继承人”是件好事。有一个不太受欢迎但同样重要的观点是，有时认识到自己不仅是祖先，而且还是远古的祖先，这对他是有好处的。对他来说，怀疑自己是否是英雄，并体验到自己是否不是太阳神话的高尚怀疑是有好处的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("The matters which most thoroughly evoke this sense of the abiding childhood of the world are those which are really fresh, abrupt and inventive in any age; and if we were asked what was the best proof of this adventurous youth in the nineteenth century we should say, with all respect to its portentous sciences and philosophies, that it was to be found in the rhymes of Mr. Edward Lear and in the literature of nonsense. 'The Dong with the Luminous Nose,' at least, is original, as the first ship and the first plough were original.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("最能彻底唤起世人永恒的童年感的事物，是那些在任何时代都真正新鲜、突然且富有创造性的事物；如果我们被问到什么是十九世纪这个富有冒险精神的年轻人的最好证据，尽管尊重其令人惊奇的科学和哲学，我们应该说，它可以在爱德华·李尔先生的韵律和无稽之谈的文学中找到。至少，“夜光鼻侗”是原创的，就像第一艘船和第一把犁是原创的一样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("It is true in a certain sense that some of the greatest writers the world has seen—Aristophanes, Rabelais and Sterne—have written nonsense; but unless we are mistaken, it is in a widely different sense. The nonsense of these men was satiric—that is to say, symbolic; it was a kind of exuberant capering round a discovered truth. There is all the difference in the world between the instinct of satire, which, seeing in the Kaiser's moustaches something typical of him, draws them continually larger and larger; and the instinct of nonsense which, for no reason whatever, imagines what those moustaches would look like on the present Archbishop of Canterbury if he grew them in a fit of absence of mind. We incline to think that no age except our own could have understood that the Quangle-Wangle meant absolutely nothing, and the Lands of the Jumblies were absolutely nowhere. We fancy that if the account of the knave's trial in 'Alice in Wonderland' had been published in the seventeenth century it would have been bracketed with Bunyan's 'Trial of Faithful' as a parody on the State prosecutions of the time. We fancy that if 'The Dong with the Luminous Nose' had appeared in the same period everyone would have called it a dull satire on Oliver Cromwell.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("从某种意义上说，世界上一些最伟大的作家——阿里斯托芬、拉伯雷和斯特恩——确实写过胡言乱语；但除非我们弄错了，否则它的含义截然不同。这些人的胡言乱语是讽刺性的——也就是说，是象征性的；这是一种围绕已发现的真理的疯狂跳跃。讽刺的本能是天壤之别，讽刺的本能在皇帝的胡子上看到了他的典型特征，于是把它们不断地画得越来越大；还有无意义的本能，无缘无故地想象，如果现在的坎特伯雷大主教心不在焉地留胡子，他会是什么样子。我们倾向于认为，除了我们这个时代之外，没有哪个时代能够理解Quangle-Wangle绝对没有任何意义，而Jumblings之地绝对不存在。我们认为，如果《爱丽丝梦游仙境》中对无赖审判的记述在十七世纪出版，它就会与班扬的《忠实审判》放在一起，作为对当时国家起诉的戏仿。我们想象，如果《发光鼻子的侗》出现在同一时期，大家都会说它是对奥利弗·克伦威尔的乏味讽刺。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("It is altogether advisedly that we quote chiefly from Mr. Lear's 'Nonsense Rhymes.' To our mind he is both chronologically and essentially the father of nonsense; we think him superior to Lewis Carroll. In one sense, indeed, Lewis Carroll has a great advantage. We know what Lewis Carroll was in daily life: he was a singularly serious and conventional don, universally respected, but very much of a pedant and something of a Philistine. Thus his strange double life in earth and in dreamland emphasizes the idea that lies at the back of nonsense—the idea of escape, of escape into a world where things are not fixed horribly in an eternal appropriateness, where apples grow on pear-trees, and any odd man you meet may have three legs. Lewis Carroll, living one life in which he would have thundered morally against any one who walked on the wrong plot of grass, and another life in which he would cheerfully call the sun green and the moon blue, was, by his very divided nature, his one foot on both worlds, a perfect type of the position of modern nonsense. His Wonderland is a country populated by insane mathematicians. We feel the whole is an escape into a world of masquerade; we feel that if we could pierce their disguises, we might discover that Humpty Dumpty and the March Hare were Professors and Doctors of Divinity enjoying a mental holiday. This sense of escape is certainly less emphatic in Edward Lear, because of the completeness of his citizenship in the world of unreason. We do not know his prosaic biography as we know Lewis Carroll's. We accept him as a purely fabulous figure, on his own description of himself:")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("我们主要引用李尔先生的《无稽之谈押韵》，这完全是经过深思熟虑的。在我们看来，他无论从时间上还是本质上都是废话之父。我们认为他优于刘易斯·卡罗尔。从某种意义上说，刘易斯·卡罗尔确实具有很大的优势。我们知道刘易斯·卡罗尔在日常生活中的样子：他是一位极其严肃、传统的教授，受到普遍尊重，但非常迂腐，有点庸俗。因此，他在地球和梦境中奇怪的双重生活强调了胡言乱语背后的想法——逃避的想法，逃避到一个世界，在这个世界里，事物并没有可怕地固定在永恒的恰当性中，苹果长在梨树上，你遇到的任何奇怪的人都可能有三条腿。刘易斯·卡罗尔过着一种生活，他会在道德上对任何走在错误草地上的人进行雷鸣般的谴责，而另一种生活，他会高兴地称太阳为绿色，月亮为蓝色，由于他非常分裂的本性，他的一只脚踩在两个世界上，这是现代胡言乱语立场的完美典型。他的仙境是一个充满疯狂数学家的国家。我们觉得这一切都是为了逃避到一个化装舞会的世界；我们觉得，如果我们能识破他们的伪装，我们可能会发现矮胖子和三月兔是享受精神假期的神学教授和博士。这种逃避感在爱德华·李尔身上当然不那么明显，因为他在非理性世界中的公民身份是完整的。我们并不像了解刘易斯·卡罗尔那样平淡无奇的传记。我们把他视为一个纯粹的神话人物，根据他自己的描述：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("While Lewis Carroll's Wonderland is purely intellectual, Lear introduces quite another element—the element of the poetical and even emotional. Carroll works by the pure reason, but this is not so strong a contrast; for, after all, mankind in the main has always regarded reason as a bit of a joke. Lear introduces his unmeaning words and his amorphous creatures not with the pomp of reason, but with the romantic prelude of rich hues and haunting rhythms.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("虽然刘易斯·卡罗尔的仙境纯粹是智力性的，但李尔王引入了另一种元素——诗意甚至情感的元素。卡罗尔的工作是出于纯粹的理性，但这并不是那么强烈的对比；毕竟，人类总体上一直将理性视为一个笑话。李尔在介绍他毫无意义的话语和他那无定形的生物时，并不是以理性的盛况，而是以丰富的色调和令人难以忘怀的节奏的浪漫前奏。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("is an entirely different type of poetry to that exhibited in 'Jabberwocky.' Carroll, with a sense of mathematical neatness, makes his whole poem a mosaic of new and mysterious words. But Edward Lear, with more subtle and placid effrontery, is always introducing scraps of his own elvish dialect into the middle of simple and rational statements, until we are almost stunned into admitting that we know what they mean. There is a genial ring of commonsense about such lines as,")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("是与“Jabberwocky”中所展示的完全不同的诗歌类型。卡罗尔具有数学的简洁感，使他的整首诗成为新奇而神秘的词语的镶嵌。但爱德华·李尔却以更加微妙和平静的厚颜无耻，总是在简单而理性的陈述中引入他自己的精灵方言的片段，直到我们几乎惊讶地承认我们知道它们的意思。关于诸如此类的台词，有一种亲切的常识，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("which is beyond the reach of Carroll. The poet seems so easy on the matter that we are almost driven to pretend that we see his meaning, that we know the peculiar difficulties of a Pobble, that we are as old travellers in the 'Gromboolian Plain' as he is.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("这超出了卡罗尔的能力范围。诗人在这个问题上似乎很轻松，以至于我们几乎被迫假装我们明白了他的意思，我们知道波布尔的特殊困难，我们和他一样是“格伦布尔平原”的老旅行者。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("Our claim that nonsense is a new literature (we might almost say a new sense) would be quite indefensible if nonsense were nothing more than a mere aesthetic fancy. Nothing sublimely artistic has ever arisen out of mere art, any more than anything essentially reasonable has ever arisen out of the pure reason. There must always be a rich moral soil for any great aesthetic growth. The principle of art for art's sake is a very good principle if it means that there is a vital distinction between the earth and the tree that has its roots in the earth; but it is a very bad principle if it means that the tree could grow just as well with its roots in the air. Every great literature has always been allegorical—allegorical of some view of the whole universe. The 'Iliad' is only great because all life is a battle, the 'Odyssey' because all life is a journey, the Book of Job because all life is a riddle. There is one attitude in which we think that all existence is summed up in the word 'ghosts'; another, and somewhat better one, in which we think it is summed up in the words 'A Midsummer Night's Dream.' Even the vulgarest melodrama or detective story can be good if it expresses something of the delight in sinister possibilities—the healthy lust for darkness and terror which may come on us any night in walking down a dark lane. If, therefore, nonsense is really to be the literature of the future, it must have its own version of the Cosmos to offer; the world must not only be the tragic, romantic, and religious, it must be nonsensical also. And here we fancy that nonsense will, in a very unexpected way, come to the aid of the spiritual view of things. Religion has for centuries been trying to make men exult in the 'wonders' of creation, but it has forgotten that a thing cannot be completely wonderful so long as it remains sensible. So long as we regard a tree as an obvious thing, naturally and reasonably created for a giraffe to eat, we cannot properly wonder at it. It is when we consider it as a prodigious wave of the living soil sprawling up to the skies for no reason in particular that we take off our hats, to the astonishment of the park-keeper. Everything has in fact another side to it, like the moon, the patroness of nonsense. Viewed from that other side, a bird is a blossom broken loose from its chain of stalk, a man a quadruped begging on its hind legs, a house a gigantesque hat to cover a man from the sun, a chair an apparatus of four wooden legs for a cripple with only two.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("如果无意义只不过是一种审美幻想，那么我们关于无意义是一种新文学（我们几乎可以说是一种新意义）的主张就完全站不住脚了。纯粹的艺术从未产生过任何崇高的艺术，正如纯粹理性从未产生过任何本质上合理的东西一样。任何伟大的审美成长都必须有丰富的道德土壤。为艺术而艺术的原则是一个非常好的原则，如果它意味着大地和扎根于大地的树之间存在着至关重要的区别；但如果这意味着树的根在空中也能生长得很好，那就是一个非常糟糕的原则。每一部伟大的文学作品总是寓言性的——寓言着整个宇宙的某种观点。 《伊利亚特》之所以伟大，是因为所有的生命都是一场战斗；《奥德赛》之所以伟大，是因为所有的生命都是一段旅程；《约伯记》之所以伟大，是因为所有的生命都是一个谜语。有一种态度认为，所有的存在都可以用“鬼魂”这个词来概括。另一种是更好的，我们认为它可以用“仲夏夜之梦”来概括。即使是最庸俗的情节剧或侦探故事，如果它表达了对险恶可能性的某种喜悦——对黑暗和恐怖的健康欲望，这种欲望可能在任何一个晚上走在黑暗的小路上时出现在我们身上，那么它也可以是好的。因此，如果无稽之谈真的成为未来的文学，它就必须提供自己的宇宙版本；这个世界不仅是悲剧性的、浪漫的、宗教性的，而且还必须是荒谬的。在这里，我们想象废话会，在非常意想不到的方式，来帮助灵性看待事物的观点。几个世纪以来，宗教一直试图让人们对创造的“奇迹”感到欣喜，但它忘记了，只要事物保持理智，它就不可能是完全美妙的。只要我们认为一棵树是显而易见的东西，是为长颈鹿吃而自然而合理地创造出来的，我们就不能适当地对它感到惊讶。当我们认为它是生机勃勃的土壤无缘无故地蔓延到天空的巨大波浪时，我们才会脱下帽子，令公园管理员感到惊讶。事实上，一切事物都有它的另一面，就像月亮一样，它是胡言乱语的守护神。从另一边看，一只鸟是一朵从花茎上挣脱出来的花朵，一个人是一个用后腿乞讨的四足动物，一座房子是一顶巨大的帽子，为一个人遮挡阳光，一把椅子是一个有四个木腿的装置，供只有两个的残疾人使用。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("This is the side of things which tends most truly to spiritual wonder. It is significant that in the greatest religious poem existent, the Book of Job, the argument which convinces the infidel is not (as has been represented by the merely rational religionism of the eighteenth century) a picture of the ordered beneficence of the Creation; but, on the contrary, a picture of the huge and undecipherable unreason of it. 'Hast Thou sent the rain upon the desert where no man is?' This simple sense of wonder at the shapes of things, and at their exuberant independence of our intellectual standards and our trivial definitions, is the basis of spirituality as it is the basis of nonsense. Nonsense and faith (strange as the conjunction may seem) are the two supreme symbolic assertions of the truth that to draw out the soul of things with a syllogism is as impossible as to draw out Leviathan with a hook. The well-meaning person who, by merely studying the logical side of things, has decided that 'faith is nonsense,' does not know how truly he speaks; later it may come back to him in the form that nonsense is faith.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("这是事物最容易产生精神奇迹的一面。值得注意的是，在现存最伟大的宗教诗歌《约伯记》中，使异教徒信服的论点并不是（如十八世纪纯粹理性的宗教主义所代表的那样）造物的有序仁慈的图画；相反，它是对创造物的有序仁慈的描述。但恰恰相反，这是一幅巨大的、难以理解的不合理的景象。 “你把雨水降到了无人居住的沙漠吗？”这种对事物形状的简单好奇感，以及它们对我们的智力标准和琐碎定义的强烈独立性，是灵性的基础，也是胡言乱语的基础。胡言乱语和信仰（尽管这两个组合看起来很奇怪）是真理的两个最高象征性断言，即用三段论引出事物的灵魂就像用钩子引出利维坦一样不可能。一个善意的人，仅仅通过研究事物的逻辑方面，就认定“信仰是无稽之谈”，但他并不知道他所说的有多真实；后来他可能会以“胡言乱语就是信仰”的形式回到他的脑海中。")]
